#---
# purpose: "integer overflow inside a guard makes the guard false, on every door"
# defer: "integer overflow inside a guard wraps or traps instead of making the clause false"
#---
#
# # guard_overflow
#
# fz integers are 64-bit, so `9223372036854775807 + 1` has no answer. In a
# body that is the `:badarith` fault. In a guard it is the same failure the
# other arithmetic failures are: the guard is false and the clause does not
# match. Elixir has unbounded integers and no such case, so this fixture has
# no oracle; the rule is fz's own and is pinned here.
defp plus_one(x) when x + 1 > 0, do: :matched
defp plus_one(_x), do: :fell_through

defp negated(x) when -x > 0, do: :matched
defp negated(_x), do: :fell_through

defp doubled(x) when x * 2 > 0, do: :matched
defp doubled(_x), do: :fell_through

def main() do
  dbg(plus_one(9223372036854775807))
  dbg(plus_one(9223372036854775806))
  dbg(negated(-9223372036854775807 - 1))
  dbg(negated(-5))
  dbg(doubled(4611686018427387904))
  dbg(doubled(4611686018427387903))
end
